- How would you handle varying campaign setup costs or penalties per segment length?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This classic problem evaluates your ability to design clean DP states for interval selection with reconstruction requirements. It strikes the right balance—accessible to beginners while effectively testing state structuring and backtracking skills.

## Key Concepts
- **dp[i][t]** manages selections across the first i elements with at most t subarrays
- **local[i][t]** distinguishes between extending an existing subarray and starting a new one
- Backtracking through the DP table to reconstruct segment boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSubarrays(nums, k) {
    if (!nums.length || k <= 0) return { maxSum: 0, subarrays: [] };

    const n = nums.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const endIdx = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
            local[i][t] = Math.max(
                local[i - 1][t] + nums[i - 1],
                dp[i - 1][t - 1] + nums[i - 1]
            );
            if (local[i][t] === dp[i - 1][t - 1] + nums[i - 1]) {
                endIdx[i][t] = i - 1;
            }
            dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t] >= local[i][t] ? dp[i - 1][t] : local[i][t];
        }
    }

    const subarrays = [];
    let j = n, t = k;
    while (t > 0 && j > 0) {
        if (endIdx[j][t] !== -1) {
            let start = endIdx[j][t];
            while (start > 0 && endIdx[start][t] !== -1) start--;
            subarrays.unshift([start, endIdx[j][t]]);
            j = start;
            t--;
        } else {
            break;
        }
    }

    return { maxSum: dp[n][k], subarrays };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums, base cases, and reconstruction, plus discussion of optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t] for first i elements into t segments
- Prefix sums for O(1) segment sums
- Quadratic segment cost and optimization (CHT or Divide-and-Conquer)
- Reconstructing segment boundaries with a prev table
- Time/space trade-offs and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k && t <= i; t++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const diff = S[i] - S[j];
        const cost = diff * diff;
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + cost;
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { cost: dp[n][k] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: block value defined as distinct counts, forcing explicit precomputation and a clear reconstruction path. Tests DP over prefixes with segment costs that depend on subarray content, not just sums.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix DP with exact block count
- Precompute interval distinct counts C[l][r]
- Reconstruct endpoints via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
// DP sketch for distinct-count blocks
let n = A.length;
let C = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
for (let L=0; L<n; L++) {
  let seen = new Map();
  let d = 0;
  for (let R=L; R<n; R++) {
    if (!seen.has(A[R])) { seen.set(A[R],1); d++; }
    C[L][R] = d;
  }
}
let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
let par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let p=t-1; p<=i-1; p++) {
      let val = dp[p][t-1] + C[p][i-1];
      if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle adds a cooldown constraint to the classic k-transaction DP, testing multi-state reasoning and recovery of the trade sequence.

## Key Concepts
- DP with cooldown using rest/hold/sold states
- Tracking transaction counts and path reconstruction
- Space optimization to O(k)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitWithCooldown(prices, k) {
  const n = prices.length;
  const INF = -1e15;
  const rest = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const hold = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const sold = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  rest[0][0] = 0;
  hold[0][0] = -prices[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      rest[i][t] = Math.max(rest[i - 1][t], sold[i - 1][t]);
      hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i - 1][t], rest[i - 1][t] - prices[i]);
      if (t > 0) sold[i][t] = hold[i - 1][t - 1] + prices[i];
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return Math.max(...rest[n - 1]);
}
```

