- How would you extract the exact trade days from the DP tables?
- How would the solution change if cooldown length varied to c days?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a range-based segment cost and path reconstruction, a common advanced pattern not covered yet.

## Key Concepts
- Contiguous partitioning
- Range query (max-min)
- DP with backtracking
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  // base
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    let mx = -Infinity, mn = Infinity;
    for (let p = i - 1; p >= 0; p--) {
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[p]);
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[p]);
      dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i)) - Math.min(...A.slice(0, i));
    }
  }
  // transitions would fill dp[i][t] and cut[i][t]
  return { cost: dp[n][k], partition: cut };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize range(p, i-1) queries to O(1) per transition?
- How would you adapt for 1-indexed arrays or allow at most k segments?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests designing a DP over a scan of the array with extra dimensions to enforce fixed-length, non-overlapping subsequences and reconstructing the chosen indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP over positions with two resource dimensions: completed subsequences and in-progress length
- Fixed-length subsequences ensure non-overlap via r reset on completion
- Backtracking reconstructs actual indices

## Code Example
